<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/articles-metadata.owl> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                                                             dc:title "TARA Standard Articles Metadata Core" ;
                                                                             rdfs:comment """Purpose: This ontology defines the data schema and LLM extraction prompts for the TARA Articles Knowledgebase, a structured, queryable repository of metadata extracted from full-text acupuncture clinical trial publications.

Ontology Content:

- Three core classes: Acupuncture Research Study (the trial itself), Acupuncture Study Publication (the reporting article), and Acupuncture Study OCSI Assessment (its structured reporting-quality assessment).

- Three metadata branches, each with its own field-level annotation properties: Bibliographic Metadata, Acupuncture Study Metadata, and OCSI Item Scoring Metadata (27 items scored per the Oregon CONSORT STRICTA Instrument).

- Controlled vocabularies for study design categories, article reporting types, and standardized acupoint/condition terms, each aligned to external sources where available.

- A general and a per-field/per-item LLM extraction prompt (JSON) attached directly to the relevant annotation property for every extractable field, so extraction pipelines and spreadsheet templates can be generated automatically from skos:altLabel without hand-maintained mappings.

- Restricted datatypes (e.g., ocsiItemScore, articleSectionType, ocsiItemNumber) enforcing valid value ranges for scored and categorical fields."""@en ,
                                                                                          """The ontology includes following classes with a rich set of metadata annotation properties:
- Acupuncture Research Study: the physical execution of an acupuncture clinical trial, from participant recruitment and baseline grouping through administration of real or sham acupuncture procedures to final endpoint evaluation
- Acupuncture Study Publication: the documentary artifact (e.g., journal articles) that reports on an acupuncture trial study
- Acupuncture Study OCSI Assessment: the structured quality assessment of acupuncture trial reporting, based on the Oregon CONSORT STRICTA Instrument (OCSI)

Metadata for each publication instance is captured through a standard set of annotation properties organized into three categories:
- Bibliographic Metadata: article title, authorship, source, and publication identifiers
- Study Metadata: intervention design, participant characteristics, sample size, outcome measures, and study outcomes
- OCSI Item Scoring Metadata: compliance with OCSI criteria for evaluating the completeness and quality of acupuncture trial reporting"""@en ;
                                                                             owl:versionInfo "1.0.0"@en .

