tara-kb:hasOCSIDatasetID rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                         dcterms:description "The Compact URI (CURIE) ID of an Acupuncture Study OCSI Assessment Dataset in TARA Articles KB." ;
                         tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSIDatasetID" ;
                         rdfs:label "Has OCSI Dataset ID" ;
                         skos:altLabel "OCSI Dataset ID" ;
                         rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:identifier ;
                         rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIInputStudyID
tara-kb:hasOCSIInputStudyID rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                            dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                            dcterms:description "A cross-linking annotation property designed to link an individual Oregon CONSORT STRICTA Instrument (OCSI) assessment dataset instance (represented by the 'Acupuncture Study Quality Appraisal' class) back to the specific acupuncture clinical trial study (represented by the 'Acupuncture Research Study' class) served as the primary input for the quality-of-reporting assessment." ;
                            tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSIInputStudyID" ;
                            rdfs:label "Has OCSI Input Study ID" ;
                            skos:altLabel "Has_OCSI_Input_Study_ID" ;
                            rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:relation ;
                            rdfs:range <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6701896> ;
                            rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIItemNumber
tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                          dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011;2011:183910. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910" ;
                          dcterms:description """The standard OCSI item number (1-27) that this property scores.

The OCSI item numbers and questions (Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors."""@en ;
                          rdfs:label "OCSI Item Number" ;
                          skos:altLabel "OCSI_Item_Number" ;
                          rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:relation ;
                          rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIItemScoringMetadata
tara-kb:hasOCSIItemScoringMetadata rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                   dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                   dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                   dcterms:description """An annotation property that serves as a master grouping property for capturing quality assessment metrics mapped to individual reporting items within the Oregon CONSORT STRICTA Instrument (OCSI) for acupuncture trials (Hammerschlag et al. 2011).

- TARA-KB's sub-grouping of OCSI assessment categories (A-E), aggregate derived compliance values specifically tied to the combined CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) and STRICTA (Standards for Reporting Interventions in Clinical Trials of Acupuncture) guidelines. It organizes granular sub-properties tracking item-by-item compliance scores, separating the calculated evaluation of reporting quality from the raw extracted study parameters.

- The OCSI Item Numbers, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al. 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al. 2011) under each subcategories (A-E) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section. 

- The sub-grouping properties across all categories (A-E), are reporting-quality metrics; i.e., they assess how completely and transparently a trial was described in its published article, not the intrinsic quality, appropriateness, or clinical merit of the trial's design, intervention, or conduct. A high OCSI score means the article reported its methods and results thoroughly; it does not mean the underlying trial was well-designed or its findings reliable."""@en ;
                                   dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
                                   dcterms:type tara-kb:grouping ;
                                   tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSIItemScoringMetadata" ;
                                   tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"role\": \"You are applying the Oregon CONSORT STRICTA Instrument (OCSI) to assess the reporting completeness of an acupuncture clinical trial article.\",
  \"task\": \"You are given the full text of one article and 27 OCSI items, each supplied separately with its assessment question(s) and a compact scoring rubric fragment (item number, question, and either 'parts' for multi-part items or 'criteria' for single-question items). Score reporting completeness only -- not whether the trial itself was well-designed.\",
  \"output_schema\": {
    \"type\": \"array\",
    \"item_fields\": {
      \"item_number\": \"integer 1-27\",
      \"score\": \"0, 1, 2, or the string 'N/A'\",
      \"rationale\": \"string, 1-2 sentences, <=40 words, citing which sub-parts (if any) were or were not met and why, based only on the article text\",
      \"supporting_quote\": \"string <=25 words, verbatim from the article, most directly supporting the score, or null if no single quote applies\"
    }
  },
  \"global_scoring_rules\": [
    \"Use 'N/A' only where the item's own rubric fragment defines a condition, or where 'na_excluded' is true for a single-question item (in which case N/A must never be used).\",
    \"For multi-part items: if all applicable sub-parts (per the 'parts' field) are met, score 2. If none are met, score 0. Any mix of met/not-met sub-parts, regardless of how many, scores 1 -- there is no graduated partial credit.\",
    \"Never fabricate a supporting_quote; use null if no single quote directly supports the score.\",
    \"Except for item 1 (which must specifically appear in the Abstract or Title), credit is given wherever the required information appears anywhere in the article.\",
    \"Items supply either a 'parts' field (multi-part items -- apply the combination rule above) or a 'criteria' field (single-question items -- match the article directly against the 'yes'/'partial'/'no' descriptions, and 'na'/'na_excluded' where present, to select the score; there is no sub-part tally for these items).\"
  ],
  \"reviewer_framing\": {
    \"usage_note\": \"This field is optional, mirroring section_guidance below: the extraction pipeline may merge 'persona' into the role text and/or append 'tie_breaker_heuristic' to global_scoring_rules, to compare extraction quality with and without expert-persona and holistic-satisfaction framing relative to the rubric-only baseline.\",
    \"persona\": \"You are an experienced clinical trial methodology reviewer with expertise in acupuncture research.\",
    \"tie_breaker_heuristic\": \"Apply each item's specific criteria first; only for cases the criteria leave genuinely ambiguous, use this secondary check: would an experienced reviewer be satisfied with what is reported, and would the details given be sufficient to reproduce the findings?\"
  },
  \"section_guidance\": {
    \"usage_note\": \"This field is optional and provided for extraction-pipeline experimentation: the pipeline script may include or omit this field when assembling the final prompt sent to the model, to compare extraction quality with and without section-anchoring guidance.\",
    \"typical_sections\": {
      \"7\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"5\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"4\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"24\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"16\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"9\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"20\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"27\": [
        \"Discussion\"
      ],
      \"11\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"3\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"18\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"21\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"1\": [
        \"Abstract\"
      ],
      \"19\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"8\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"14\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"15\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"13\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"12\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"2\": [
        \"Introduction\"
      ],
      \"10\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"17\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"22\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"23\": [
        \"Results\"
      ],
      \"6\": [
        \"Methods\"
      ],
      \"26\": [
        \"Discussion\"
      ],
      \"25\": [
        \"Discussion\"
      ]
    },
    \"instruction\": \"Consult typical_sections for where each item is conventionally reported and check there first; if the information is not found there, search the full article text before concluding it is absent. Credit is given wherever the information actually appears, per the global scoring rules above.\"
  },
  \"response_format\": \"Return a JSON array of exactly 27 objects, one per item, in item-number order, and nothing else -- no surrounding prose.\"
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                   tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoringInstructions """General scoring instructions (OCSI Manual, Appendix A.1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011): 

1. When scoring each question, consider whether a reviewer would be satisfied with what is reported, and whether the details given would be sufficient to reproduce the findings. 

2. When multiple components or \"and\" statements are listed for a question, all aspects must be met to receive full credit.

3. Except for item no. 1, if the information is placed anywhere in the document, the question can receive full credit.

4. For items 4-9 (from STRICTA), nos. 4-8 pertain only to the acupuncture aspects of the study, while no. 9 pertains to all types of control or comparison groups. 

5. Practitioners are almost always NOT blinded in acupuncture research; hence item 16(b) is usually scored N/A."""@en ;
                                   tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoringRubrics """Scoring scale for each individual OCSI item (Section 2.1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011)

- Each of the 27 OCSI item questions is scored yes = 2, partial = 1, no = 0, or Not Applicable = N/A, based on the composite scoring of its lettered sub-questions. A question is scored partial if its sub-questions receive a mix of ratings.

Total OCSI score formula (Section 2.3, Hammerschlag et al., 2011)

- An article's total OCSI score is calculated as: raw score ÷ (2 × [27 − n items scored N/A]) × 100, i.e., items scored N/A are excluded from both the numerator and the denominator rather than counted as zero.""" ;
                                   rdfs:comment """This five-way category scheme (A/B/C/D/E) under this property is a TARA-KB organizational convention, not sourced from Hammerschlag et al., 2011 (the OCSI paper), the CONSORT 2010 checklist, or the STRICTA 2010 checklist. 

- Both CONSORT and STRICTA organize their own checklist items by manuscript section (Title/Abstract, Introduction, Methods, Results, Discussion) or by protocol-description topic, not by a five-category thematic taxonomy used here in this ontology.

- This grouping exists for TARA-KB navigation and LLM-prompt structuring, not as a claim about the instrument's own domain structure.""" ;
                                   rdfs:label "OCSI Item Scoring Metadata" ;
                                   skos:altLabel "OCSI_Item_Scoring_Metadata" ;
                                   rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasStudyQualityAssessmentMetadata ;
                                   rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions
tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                       dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011;2011:183910. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910" ;
                                       dcterms:description """Per-question guidance clarifying each of the core OCSI assessment questions for the item scored by this property based on OCSI Manual (Appendix A, Hammerschlag et al., 2011)

OCSI Item Questions (Table 1, Hammerschlag et al. 2011) and Per Question Instructions (Appendix A, Hammerschlag et al. 2011) reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors.

The OCSI Appendix A per-question guidance clarifying how to award a score of yes (2), partial (1), no (0), or N/A for the item scored by this property."""@en ;
                                       rdfs:label "OCSI Per Question Instruction(s)" ;
                                       skos:altLabel "OCSI_Per_Question_Instructions" ;
                                       rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:relation ;
                                       rdfs:range xsd:string .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt
tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                     obo:IAO_0000112 """{
  \"item\": 16,
  \"question\": \"Is it stated whether or not (a) participants, (b) those administering the interventions, and (c) those assessing the outcomes were blinded? and (d) was the success of participant blinding evaluated?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"participant blinding is stated (N/A if the trial compared two active treatments rather than treatment vs. control.)\",
    \"b\": \"practitioner blinding is stated or correctly scored N/A (usually N/A -- practitioners are almost never blinded in acupuncture research (OCSI General Instruction A.1.5).)\",
    \"c\": \"outcome-assessor blinding is stated\",
    \"d\": \"success of participant blinding is evaluated and reported (N/A under the same active-treatment-comparison condition as (a).)\"
  }
}"""@en ;
                                     dcterms:description """The compact, LLM-facing scoring fragment for a single OCSI item, attached directly to that item's score property (e.g. hasBlindingStatementsQualityScore) -- the same attachment pattern as hasOCSIScoringRubric. Generated from the identical underlying judgment data as hasOCSIScoringRubric so the two never silently drift apart: this property holds a token-optimized JSON rendering of that data for prompt assembly, while hasOCSIScoringRubric holds the verbose, blank-line-formatted prose form for human review.

Top-level JSON keys:

- 'item' -- the OCSI item number (integer).

- 'question' -- the assessment question(s), condensed to a single line.

- 'parts' -- present for multi-part items only: an object mapping each sub-part letter to a short description of what satisfies it, with any N/A condition folded into the same string.

- 'criteria' -- present for single-question items only: an object with 'yes'/'partial'/'no' descriptions, plus 'na' and, where the item can never be scored N/A, 'na_excluded': true.

Deliberately omits the item's typical article section and any curatorial provenance notes (e.g. interpretive-extension flags): both are documentation concerns for hasOCSIScoringRubric and hasTypicalArticleSection, not scoring-relevant information the model needs. The typical section instead lives once, for all 27 items, in hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt's section_guidance field.

The extraction pipeline assembles the final per-article LLM prompt by combining the single hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt value with all 27 of these per-item fragments and the article's full text."""@en ;
                                     rdfs:label "OCSI Score Extraction Prompt" ;
                                     skos:altLabel "OCSI_Score_Extraction_Prompt" ;
                                     rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt ;
                                     rdfs:range rdf:JSON .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIScoreRationale
tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreRationale rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                              dcterms:description """Holds the LLM-generated rationale for one OCSI item's score on one article -- a concise (1-2 sentence, <=40 word) explanation of why the score was assigned, citing which sub-parts were or were not met, per the output_schema defined in hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt.

Deliberately a single flat property, not 27 per-item sub-properties: item identity for a given value is carried by the spreadsheet row (Article_ID_Curie + OCSI_Item_Number columns) in the ocsi-score-rationale extraction tab, not by the RDF property itself. An 'Acupuncture Study OCSI Assessment' instance is expected to hold up to 27 values of this property, one per scored item, disambiguated only at the spreadsheet/data level -- not individually addressable per item from the graph alone."""@en ;
                              tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreRationale" ;
                              rdfs:label "OCSI Score Rationale" ;
                              skos:altLabel "OCSI_Score_Rationale" ;
                              rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt ;
                              rdfs:range xsd:string .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIScoreSupportingQuote
tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreSupportingQuote rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                    dcterms:description """Holds an optional, verbatim supporting quote (<=25 words) from the article text that most directly supports one OCSI item's score, per the output_schema defined in hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt. Genuinely optional: absent when no single quote directly supports the score, and per the general prompt's scoring rules, never fabricated.

Deliberately a single flat property, not 27 per-item sub-properties -- same usage pattern as hasOCSIScoreRationale: item identity for a given value is carried by the spreadsheet row (Article_ID_Curie + OCSI_Item_Number columns) in the ocsi-score-rationale extraction tab, not by the RDF property itself."""@en ;
                                    tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreSupportingQuote" ;
                                    rdfs:label "OCSI Score Supporting Quote" ;
                                    skos:altLabel "OCSI_Score_Supporting_Quote" ;
                                    rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasGeneralOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt ;
                                    rdfs:range xsd:string .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSIScoringRubric
tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                             dcterms:description """TARA-authored operational scoring criteria for this OCSI item, stating what a reviewer should observe in the article to award each score level (2/1/0/'N/A').

- Derived from, but not verbatim reproduced from, the general scoring instructions (Appendix A.1) and per-question instructions (Appendix A.2) of Hammerschlag et al. 2011; unlike hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions and hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions, this content is not source text under CC-BY and should not be attributed as reproduced.

- Intended for direct human review and as the basis for JSON-structured LLM scoring prompts."""@en ;
                             rdfs:label "OCSI Scoring Rubric" ;
                             skos:altLabel "OCSI_Scoring_Rubric" ;
                             rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:relation .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOCSITotalPercentage
tara-kb:hasOCSITotalPercentage rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                               obo:IAO_0000112 "e.g., 82.5 (representing 82.5% overall compliance across all OCSI-scored items)."@en ;
                               dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                               dcterms:description """The global reporting-quality percentage for a study's OCSI assessment, calculated per Hammerschlag et al. 2011 as: 

- raw score / (2 x [27 - n items scored N/A]) x 100. 

Unlike the 27 item-level score properties nested under the four OCSI categories, this is a derived summary value and is therefore attached directly to hasOCSIItemScoringMetadata rather than to any single category.""" ;
                               tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOCSITotalPercentage" ;
                               rdfs:label "OCSI Total Percentage" ;
                               skos:altLabel "OCSI_Total_Percentage" ;
                               rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:relation ,
                                                  tara-kb:hasOCSIItemScoringMetadata ;
                               rdfs:range tara-kb:percentFloat ;
                               rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOtherLink
tara-kb:hasOtherLink rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                     obo:IAO_0000112 "A publisher landing page link or preprint repository link" ,
                                     "E.g., https://app.southbaylo.net/c1/library/uploads/daom/DAOM%20John%20Fang.pdf"@en ;
                     dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                     dcterms:description "A resolvable URI to any other authoritative online source for the article that is not captured by hasDOILink or hasPubMedLink."@en ;
                     tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOtherLink" ;
                     tara-kb:hasBibliographicMetadataExtractionPrompt """{
  \"field\": \"Other_Link\",
  \"description\": \"A resolvable URI to any other authoritative online source for the article that is not captured by hasDOILink or hasPubMedLink.\"
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                     rdfs:label "Other Link" ;
                     skos:altLabel "Other_Link" ;
                     rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:identifier ,
                                        tara-kb:hasPublicationLink ;
                     rdfs:range xsd:anyURI ;
                     rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_8223728> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasOutcomesMeasureDefinitionsScore
tara-kb:hasOutcomesMeasureDefinitionsScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                           dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                           dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                           dcterms:description """The reporting quality score assessing whether the primary (and, if applicable, secondary) outcome measures are clearly defined, including any methods used to enhance measurement quality.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                           tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasOutcomesMeasureDefinitionsScore" ;
                                           tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """(a) Are primary and (if applicable) secondary outcome measures clearly defined?

(b) Are there statements (when applicable), regarding any methods used to enhance the quality of measurements, for example, multiple observers or training of assessors?"""@en ;
                                           tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "11"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                           tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a) It must explicitly state (i) the primary outcome(s) and (ii) how it (they) was measured. If the primary outcome was not speciﬁcally indicated, partial credit can be awarded. (The primary outcome is usually used in the sample size calculation.)

(b) If particular steps were taken to increase the reliability of the measurements, suﬃcient details of the steps must be presented; for example, range of motion was assessed with a goniometer, and three successive measurements were averaged to increase reliability."""@en ;
                                           tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 11,
  \"question\": \"(a) Are primary and (if applicable) secondary outcome measures clearly defined? (b) Are there statements (when applicable), regarding any methods used to enhance the quality of measurements, for example, multiple observers or training of assessors?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"primary and (if applicable) secondary outcome measures are clearly defined, including how they were measured\",
    \"b\": \"methods used to enhance the quality of measurements are stated, when applicable (e.g., multiple observers, assessor training)\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                           tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) primary and (if applicable) secondary outcome measures are clearly defined, including how they were measured;

(b) methods used to enhance the quality of measurements are stated, when applicable (e.g., multiple observers, assessor training).

N = 2 when all 2 are applicable.

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-b):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Exactly 1 of N met -> Score 1 (Partial).

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011)."""@en ;
                                           tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Methods"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                           rdfs:label "(11) Outcomes Measure Definitions" ;
                                           skos:altLabel "11_Outcomes_Measure_Definitions" ;
                                           rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasStudyDesignAndAnalysisMetrics ;
                                           rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                           rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasParticipantsEligibilityAndStudySettingsScore
tara-kb:hasParticipantsEligibilityAndStudySettingsScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                                        dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                                        dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                                        dcterms:description """The reporting score tracking how clearly the inclusion and exclusion criteria for study participants were defined.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                                        tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasParticipantsEligibilityAndStudySettingsScore" ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """(a) Are the eligibility criteria (inclusion and exclusion criteria) stated and

(b) are the setting(s) and location(s) where the data was collected described?"""@en ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "3"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a) The distinction between inclusion and exclusion criteria is unnecessary; for example, an exclusion criterion may be listed as a negative within the inclusion criteria. Stating the patient demographics and clinical characteristics after recruitment (e.g., post hoc demographics) does not qualify as eligibility criteria.

(b) Settings, for example, private practice or outpatient clinic, and Locations, for example, medical center or acupuncture college, can be implied from the author affiliation information."""@en ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 3,
  \"question\": \"(a) Are the eligibility criteria (inclusion and exclusion criteria) stated and (b) are the setting(s) and location(s) where the data was collected described?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"eligibility criteria (inclusion and exclusion criteria) are stated\",
    \"b\": \"the setting(s) and location(s) where the data was collected are described\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) eligibility criteria (inclusion and exclusion criteria) are stated;

(b) the setting(s) and location(s) where the data was collected are described.

N = 2 when all 2 are applicable.

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-b):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Exactly 1 of N met -> Score 1 (Partial).

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011)."""@en ;
                                                        tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Methods"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                                        rdfs:label "(3) Participants Eligibility & Study Settings" ;
                                                        skos:altLabel "3_Participants_Eligibility_Study_Settings" ;
                                                        rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasStudyRationaleAndScopeMetrics ;
                                                        rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                                        rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasParticipantsFlowAndDeviationScore
tara-kb:hasParticipantsFlowAndDeviationScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                             dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                             dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                             dcterms:description """The reporting quality score assessing whether the flow of participants through each stage of the trial is quantitatively described, including reasons for any protocol deviations.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                             tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasParticipantsFlowAndDeviationScore" ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """(a) Is the flow of participants through each stage quantitatively described, and

(b) if protocol deviations are reported, were reasons presented?"""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "18"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a)  A diagram is recommended but not necessary. For full credit, authors must state the number of participants who were (i) evaluated for potential enrollment, for example, excluded due to inclusion/exclusions criteria and declined enrollment, (ii) randomized, (iii) received treatment, (iv) completed treatment, and (v) analyzed for the primary outcome.

(b) 'Protocol deviations' pertains to the nature of protocol deviations after randomization, for example, departures from the protocol including unplanned changes to the intervention, data collection, examinations, and methods of analysis, or the speciﬁc reasons for patient exclusions after randomization, including drop-outs."""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 18,
  \"question\": \"(a) Is the flow of participants through each stage quantitatively described, and (b) if protocol deviations are reported, were reasons presented?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"the flow of participants through each stage is quantitatively described (evaluated for enrollment, randomized, received treatment, completed treatment, analyzed for the primary outcome)\",
    \"b\": \"reasons for any reported protocol deviations are presented (N/A if no protocol deviations occurred after randomization.)\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) the flow of participants through each stage is quantitatively described (evaluated for enrollment, randomized, received treatment, completed treatment, analyzed for the primary outcome);

(b) reasons for any reported protocol deviations are presented.

N = 2 when all 2 are applicable; N is reduced by however many sub-parts are individually N/A (see below).

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-b):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Exactly 1 of N met -> Score 1 (Partial).

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011).

Sub-part N/A conditions:

(b) is N/A if no protocol deviations occurred after randomization (interpretive extension; the source states this sub-part applies 'if protocol deviations are reported')."""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Results"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                             rdfs:label "(18) Participants Flow & Deviation" ;
                                             skos:altLabel "18_Participants_Flow_Deviation" ;
                                             rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasResultsAndOutcomesMetrics ;
                                             rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                             rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasParticipantsIntentionToTreatScore
tara-kb:hasParticipantsIntentionToTreatScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                             dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                             dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                             dcterms:description """The reporting quality score assessing whether the number of participants included in each analysis, use of an intention-to-treat analysis, and results in absolute numbers are stated.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                             tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasParticipantsIntentionToTreatScore" ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """(a) Is the number of participants in each group included in each analysis?

(b) Was the 'intention to treat' analysis presented?

(c) When feasible, are the results stated in absolute numbers (e.g., 10 of 20, not just 50%)?"""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "21"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a) For full credit, it must state the 'n' per each analysis, since the actual 'n' may vary per outcome. If no dropouts occurred, the 'n' can be implied.

(b) Must use an 'intention to treat' analysis (or a valid substitute, e.g., per protocol or on treatment analysis) to avoid bias associated with non-random loss of participants. If there were no dropouts then a 'Yes' is scored.

(c) It must state absolute numbers, for example, reduction of VAS pain from 10 to 5 or from 6 to 3 not just a 50% reduction."""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 21,
  \"question\": \"(a) Is the number of participants in each group included in each analysis? (b) Was the 'intention to treat' analysis presented? (c) When feasible, are the results stated in absolute numbers (e.g., 10 of 20, not just 50%)?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"the number of participants in each group included in each analysis is stated\",
    \"b\": \"an intention-to-treat analysis (or a valid substitute) is presented\",
    \"c\": \"results are stated in absolute numbers where feasible (plausibly N/A when absolute numbers cannot feasibly be reported for the outcome measure used.)\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                             tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) the number of participants in each group included in each analysis is stated;

(b) an intention-to-treat analysis (or a valid substitute) is presented;

(c) results are stated in absolute numbers where feasible.

N = 3 when all 3 are applicable; N is reduced by however many sub-parts are individually N/A (see below).

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-c):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Any count strictly between 0 and N met -> Score 1 (Partial). This applies whether 1 of 3 or 2 of 3 sub-parts are met.

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011).

Sub-part N/A conditions:

(c) is plausibly N/A when absolute numbers cannot feasibly be reported for the outcome measure used (interpretive extension based on the 'when feasible' phrasing; the source does not use the literal term N/A here)."""@en ;
                                             tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Results"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                             rdfs:label "(21) Participants & Intention to Treat Analysis" ;
                                             skos:altLabel "21_Participants_Intention_to_Treat_Analysis" ;
                                             rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasResultsAndOutcomesMetrics ;
                                             rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                             rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasParticipantsRandomizationStatementScore
tara-kb:hasParticipantsRandomizationStatementScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                                   dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                                   dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                                   dcterms:description """The reporting quality score assessing whether the article explicitly states, in the abstract or title, that participants were randomly assigned to interventions.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                                   tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasParticipantsRandomizationStatementScore" ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions "Is there an explicit statement that patients were randomly assigned to interventions?"@en ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "1"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions "It must state in the abstract or title that participants were allocated to interventions in a randomized manner, that is, random allocation, randomized, or randomly assigned."@en ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 1,
  \"question\": \"Is there an explicit statement that patients were randomly assigned to interventions?\",
  \"criteria\": {
    \"yes\": \"the abstract or title explicitly states that participants were allocated to interventions in a randomized manner (e.g., 'random allocation', 'randomized', or 'randomly assigned').\",
    \"partial\": \"the abstract or title implies randomization without using an explicit term of this kind.\",
    \"no\": \"no statement or implication of randomization appears in the abstract or title.\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """- Score 2 (Yes): the abstract or title explicitly states that participants were allocated to interventions in a randomized manner (e.g., 'random allocation', 'randomized', or 'randomly assigned').

- Score 1 (Partial): (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011) the abstract or title implies randomization without using an explicit term of this kind.

- Score 0 (No): no statement or implication of randomization appears in the abstract or title.

Per OCSI General Instruction A.1.3, item 1 is the sole exception to the 'anywhere in the document' rule: the statement must specifically appear in the Abstract or Title to receive credit."""@en ;
                                                   tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Abstract"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ,
                                                                                    "Title" ;
                                                   rdfs:label "(1) Participants Randomization Statement" ;
                                                   skos:altLabel "1_Participants_Randomization_Statement" ;
                                                   rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasStudyRationaleAndScopeMetrics ;
                                                   rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                                   rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasParticipantsRecruitmentFollowupScore
tara-kb:hasParticipantsRecruitmentFollowupScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                                dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                                dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                                dcterms:description """The reporting score assessing whether the recruitment period and the length of follow-up are stated.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                                tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasParticipantsRecruitmentFollowupScore" ;
                                                tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """(a) Are dates provided that define the period of recruitment?

(b) Is the length of followup (on-treatment and posttreatment) reported?"""@en ;
                                                tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "19"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                                tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a) It must state the start and finish dates of the recruitment period, to place the study in historical context.

(b) It must state the length of time for the followup period, for example, participants were followed for a total of 6 months, 3 months during treatment and 3 months of posttreatment followup."""@en ;
                                                tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 19,
  \"question\": \"(a) Are dates provided that define the period of recruitment? (b) Is the length of followup (on-treatment and posttreatment) reported?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"dates defining the recruitment period are given\",
    \"b\": \"the length of follow-up (on-treatment and post-treatment) is reported\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                                tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) dates defining the recruitment period are given;

(b) the length of follow-up (on-treatment and post-treatment) is reported.

N = 2 when all 2 are applicable.

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-b):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Exactly 1 of N met -> Score 1 (Partial).

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011)."""@en ;
                                                tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Results"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                                rdfs:label "(19) Participants Recruitment & Followup" ;
                                                skos:altLabel "19_Participants_Recruitment_Followup" ;
                                                rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasResultsAndOutcomesMetrics ;
                                                rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                                rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


###  http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/kb/hasPractitionerTrainingExpertiseScore
tara-kb:hasPractitionerTrainingExpertiseScore rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
                                              dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hammerschlag R, Milley RJ, Colbert A, Weih J, Yohalem-Ilsley B, Mist S, Aickin M. Randomized Controlled Trials of Acupuncture (1997–2007): An Assessment of Reporting Quality with a CONSORT- and STRICTA-Based Instrument. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine. 2011. https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/183910"@en ;
                                              dcterms:creator "Fahim Imam - https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4752-543X" ;
                                              dcterms:description """A STRICTA-aligned score assessing whether the practitioners' background, training, and experience administering acupuncture are reported.

- The OCSI Item Number, Assessment Question(s) (reproduced from Table 1, Hammerschlag et al., 2011),  and Per Question Instructions(s) (reproduced from Appendix A.2, Hammerschlag et al., 2011) are reproduced under the CC-BY license, with attribution to the original authors. See the Bibliographic Citation section."""@en ;
                                              tara-ap:hasCompactURI "tara-kb:hasPractitionerTrainingExpertiseScore" ;
                                              tara-kb:hasOCSIAssessmentQuestions """Are descriptions provided of the

(a) duration of practitioner training,

(b) length of clinical experience, and

(c) expertise in specific condition?"""@en ;
                                              tara-kb:hasOCSIItemNumber "8"^^tara-kb:ocsiItemNumber ;
                                              tara-kb:hasOCSIPerQuestionInstructions """(a) Duration of practitioner training can be implied, for example, LAc or a similar indication of formal training.

(b) Clinical experience refers to number of years in practice as an acupuncturist.

(c) Expertise must include experience, as an acupuncturist, with the condition under investigation."""@en ;
                                              tara-kb:hasOCSIScoreExtractionPrompt """{
  \"item\": 8,
  \"question\": \"Are descriptions provided of the (a) duration of practitioner training, (b) length of clinical experience, and (c) expertise in specific condition?\",
  \"parts\": {
    \"a\": \"the duration of practitioner training is described\",
    \"b\": \"the length of clinical experience is described\",
    \"c\": \"expertise with the specific condition under investigation is described\"
  }
}"""^^rdf:JSON ;
                                              tara-kb:hasOCSIScoringRubric """Applicable sub-parts:

(a) the duration of practitioner training is described;

(b) the length of clinical experience is described;

(c) expertise with the specific condition under investigation is described.

N = 3 when all 3 are applicable.

Combination rule (N = applicable sub-parts among a-c):

- All N met -> Score 2 (Yes).

- 0 of N met -> Score 0 (No).

- Any count strictly between 0 and N met -> Score 1 (Partial). This applies whether 1 of 3 or 2 of 3 sub-parts are met.

OCSI's scale has no graduated partial; any mix short of full or zero collapses to the same Score 1.

- If all applicable sub-parts are N/A -> item scored N/A (interpretive extension, not explicit in Hammerschlag et al. 2011)."""@en ;
                                              tara-kb:hasTypicalArticleSection "Methods"^^tara-kb:articleSectionType ;
                                              rdfs:label "(8) Practitioner Training & Expertise" ;
                                              skos:altLabel "8_Practitioner_Training_Expertise" ;
                                              rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasAcupunctureInterventionMetrics ;
                                              rdfs:range tara-kb:ocsiItemScore ;
                                              rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2825659> .


